<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> This SCP is a set of three six sided dice that, when rolled, will produce an anomalous object determined by the total of the dice. The objects seem benign at first, but once a certain condition is met, the object will manifest a negative effect.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The main bulk of the article is in the experimentation logs which detail the results of almost every die roll, with the higher rolls resulting in more dangerous effects. The &quot;almost&quot; is there because a few of the rolls were not achieved before the number 18 was rolled, which resulted in the dice becoming uncontained. Examples of dice results would be 3, which produces pizza that turns into non-poisonous spiders when bitten into, 8, which produces soap that explodes when contacting water, 16, which produces a jar of jelly which the condition to activate is yet to be found, and 18, which would produce a hand written note thanking the individual who rolled the dice for returning them, along with the dice instantaneously disappearing.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The main draw of the article is the interesting ways that all of the objects manifest their negative effects, and the resulting effects, which all have some sort of irony to them.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> One thing I especially want feedback on is your thoughts on my idea for the 18 result (though I'm not sure if that type of critic is supposed to wait until the draft phase). I want to subvert the &quot;something really bad happens when you roll 666&quot; shtick without it coming off as anticlimactic, and I'm not sure if a note from (presumably) the devil would be a good fit for that. If you have any other ideas for that I'd love to hear it, as I think a lot of what the reader takes away from the article would be reliant on how impactful that last die roll is.</p>

							</div>

								
					
