					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607758630" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607753545 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Dec 2020 06:12</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13893871/jeanne-s-home-for-strange-and-lost-creatures">Jeanne&#039;s Home for Strange and Lost Creatures</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842761">
									

<p>As a general note, minutiae details like these:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Are they choleric, sanguine, phlegmatic or melancholic? You can pull off an ensemble effect if you array them all.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>What are their DnD alignments?</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>What are their wardrobe choices and how do these reflect their character? (maybe too much detail for a file but ok for a Tale or offset).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>aren't really required, or recommended, to be included in the core concept explanation. Most readers aren't likely to be very familiar with the four temperaments/Humoralism and what defines those four typical character profiles, so inclusion of that information is more fluff text than crucial understanding material. Similarly, D&amp;D alignments don't really matter this early on in the conceptualization stage; there's no need for an author to go to the additional effort to pin down alignments for the sole sake of just being able to provide a laundry list. Clothing design specifics are pretty irrelevant unless an entity's clothing is meant to be a major plot point. During the concept review stage, the author should focus on piquing interest in the story that's being told, not building dating sim profiles for the characters.</p>

							</div>

								
					
